1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:02,002
Lesson 2.6.

2
00:00:02,382 --> 00:00:03,327
Ergodicity.

3
00:00:04,450 --> 00:00:08,636
A thermodynamic computer almost
never returns an isolated sample: it

4
00:00:08,636 --> 00:00:12,761
returns an average of many consecutive
readings of the same system.

5
00:00:13,141 --> 00:00:15,540
Does that time average
give the same as the

6
00:00:15,540 --> 00:00:17,605
average over many independent copies?

7
00:00:17,985 --> 00:00:19,438
And how much do we trust it?

8
00:00:19,818 --> 00:00:24,606
The answer has two parts: ergodicity
and the autocorrelation time.

9
00:00:24,986 --> 00:00:27,527
Without them, our error bars lie.

10
00:00:28,650 --> 00:00:30,876
Look at two particles in a double well.

11
00:00:31,256 --> 00:00:36,052
The left one has a low barrier: it
hops between wells many times, and its

12
00:00:36,052 --> 00:00:40,716
fraction of time in each well converges
to one half, like the ensemble.

13
00:00:41,096 --> 00:00:46,387
The right one has a huge barrier: in all
the time we watch it, it never crosses.

14
00:00:46,767 --> 00:00:49,922
Its time average says the
system is always in the

15
00:00:49,922 --> 00:00:52,755
right well, which is
false for the ensemble.

16
00:00:53,135 --> 00:00:55,694
In practice, a system is ergodic if we

17
00:00:55,694 --> 00:00:58,590
observe it for longer
than its mixing time.

18
00:00:59,717 --> 00:01:04,216
Even if the system is ergodic,
consecutive samples are not independent.

19
00:01:04,596 --> 00:01:09,165
The autocorrelation measures
how much the current value

20
00:01:09,165 --> 00:01:13,486
resembles the value a while
later: ρ(τ) = C(τ)/C(0).

21
00:01:13,866 --> 00:01:17,834
For the Ornstein–Uhlenbeck process
it decays as an exponential.

22
00:01:18,214 --> 00:01:20,753
The curve measured in the
simulation follows the

23
00:01:20,753 --> 00:01:23,293
theoretical exponential,
with noise in the tail.

24
00:01:23,673 --> 00:01:28,546
The area under the curve, times two,
is the integrated autocorrelation

25
00:01:28,546 --> 00:01:32,863
time: how long you must wait to
get an effectively new sample.

26
00:01:33,983 --> 00:01:37,814
If we take n samples spaced
by a step Δt, and the

27
00:01:37,814 --> 00:01:41,879
integrated autocorrelation
time, in steps, is τ_int,

28
00:01:42,259 --> 00:01:48,328
we only have about n/τ_int independent
samples: the effective sample size.

29
00:01:48,708 --> 00:01:53,725
Sampling faster than the autocorrelation
adds no new information, only copies.

30
00:01:54,105 --> 00:01:58,041
The variance of the average
is the variance of one

31
00:01:58,041 --> 00:02:01,425
sample times τ_int/n,
not times one over n.

32
00:02:02,550 --> 00:02:05,663
Let's check by repeating the
experiment two hundred times.

33
00:02:06,043 --> 00:02:09,541
Each point is the average of
one run, with its error bar.

34
00:02:09,921 --> 00:02:13,304
If we ignore the correlation,
the orange bars are

35
00:02:13,304 --> 00:02:16,479
too short: many do not
contain the true value.

36
00:02:16,859 --> 00:02:22,167
With the autocorrelation-time correction,
the green bars contain the true value

37
00:02:22,167 --> 00:02:26,804
in the expected proportion, about
ninety-five percent for two sigmas.

38
00:02:27,184 --> 00:02:31,912
A practical tool to estimate them is
the blocking method: average over blocks

39
00:02:31,912 --> 00:02:35,903
longer than the autocorrelation
and use the spread of the blocks.

40
00:02:37,017 --> 00:02:43,710
An Ornstein–Uhlenbeck process with
rate 0.1, read every 0.1 time units,

41
00:02:43,710 --> 00:02:49,649
has autocorrelation e^(−0.01 k)
between readings k steps apart.

42
00:02:50,029 --> 00:02:53,048
Its integrated time is
one plus that number over

43
00:02:53,048 --> 00:02:56,004
one minus that number:
about two hundred steps.

44
00:02:56,384 --> 00:03:00,662
With ten thousand readings, the effective
sample size is only about fifty.

45
00:03:01,043 --> 00:03:04,770
The real error bar is about fourteen
times larger than the naive one.

46
00:03:05,883 --> 00:03:08,354
In hardware this has direct consequences.

47
00:03:08,734 --> 00:03:12,211
The time the linear solver
must integrate grows with the

48
00:03:12,211 --> 00:03:16,309
correlation time, which is the
inverse of the smallest eigenvalue.

49
00:03:16,689 --> 00:03:19,168
Reading a pbit chip faster than its

50
00:03:19,168 --> 00:03:22,567
correlation time wastes
input and output energy.

51
00:03:22,947 --> 00:03:27,429
Near a phase transition, the
Gibbs correlation time blows up.

52
00:03:27,809 --> 00:03:32,431
And in every lab, error bars
must include the autocorrelation.

53
00:03:33,550 --> 00:03:38,893
The autocorrelation time is bounded
by the spectral gap from lesson 0.5.

54
00:03:39,273 --> 00:03:41,882
The autocorrelation is also the response,

55
00:03:41,882 --> 00:03:44,364
by the fluctuation–dissipation theorem.

56
00:03:44,744 --> 00:03:48,701
And in unit four we will use it
as a diagnostic for samplers.

57
00:03:49,817 --> 00:03:54,803
Frequent mistakes: using the independent-sample
formula with correlated data.

58
00:03:55,183 --> 00:03:56,879
Not discarding the transient.

59
00:03:57,259 --> 00:04:00,060
Confusing theoretical ergodicity
with practical ergodicity.

60
00:04:00,440 --> 00:04:02,574
And estimating the
integrated time by summing

61
00:04:02,574 --> 00:04:04,614
the whole noisy tail
without truncating it.

62
00:04:05,717 --> 00:04:08,754
In summary: a system is
ergodic in practice if we

63
00:04:08,754 --> 00:04:11,420
observe it for longer
than its mixing time.

64
00:04:11,800 --> 00:04:15,494
Consecutive samples are
correlated, and the integrated

65
00:04:15,494 --> 00:04:19,394
autocorrelation time says how
many are truly independent.

66
00:04:19,774 --> 00:04:24,495
The correct error bar is the square
root of the variance times τ_int over n.

67
00:04:25,617 --> 00:04:30,204
To practice: compute the integrated
time of a geometric autocorrelation.

68
00:04:30,584 --> 00:04:32,623
Get an effective sample size.

69
00:04:33,003 --> 00:04:37,552
Explain why a well with a forty k_B
T barrier is not ergodic in practice.

70
00:04:37,932 --> 00:04:39,774
And describe the blocking method.
